// ora-mod-ap — generated by tools/merge.js: src/ora-mod-ap-*.js plus matching questions from the Oracle role banks (tools/modules.js).
// Oracle Modules — Payables, Payments & Expenses. Format: [topic, question, answer, 'B'|'D']
QB['ora-mod-ap'] = (QB['ora-mod-ap'] || []).concat([
['Setup', 'What are the key Payables setups in Fusion?',
`- **Payables and Procurement options** (accounting options, prepayment, conversion rates).
- **Invoice options**, **payment options**, **tax and withholding**.
- **Invoice tolerances** and **hold/release names**.
- **Payment terms**, **distribution sets**.
- **Invoice approval** (BPM rules).
- **Payment methods**, **payment process profiles**, **disbursement bank accounts**.
- **Supplier** setups with sites and assignments.`, 'B'],

['Invoices', 'What are distribution sets and when do you use them?',
`- Templates that **automatically create invoice distributions** — full (fixed amounts/percentages) or skeleton (accounts only).
- Example: monthly utility invoice split 40/60 between two cost centers.
- Assigned to supplier sites as defaults.`, 'B'],

['Invoices', 'How do you match an invoice to a PO, a receipt and a consumption advice?',
`- **PO match**: match to PO schedules (quantity × price or amount).
- **Receipt match**: match to specific receipts (3-way), useful for price variance by receipt.
- **Consumption advice match**: for consignment inventory — matching to consumption.
- Matching option on the PO schedule determines whether AP must match to receipts or POs.`, 'D'],

['Invoices', 'What are quick credits and price corrections?',
`- **Price correction**: adjusts the unit price of a previously matched invoice without changing quantity (e.g. supplier increases price after original invoice).
- **Quick credit**: a credit memo that fully reverses an existing invoice (all lines) in one step.
- **Quantity correction** via credit memo matched to the original.`, 'D'],

['Holds', 'How do you set up hold resolution routing?',
`- Holds can route to owners via **holds resolution workflow** (e.g. price holds to buyer, quantity received holds to requester).
- Rules by hold type and attributes; notifications with actions (release, request info).
- Measure days on hold by type and owner.`, 'D'],

['Payments', 'Walk me through the components of the Fusion Payments setup.',
`- **Payment methods** (check, EFT, wire) with usage rules (by BU, currency, supplier).
- **Payment formats** (BI Publisher templates).
- **Payment process profiles**: format, transmission, grouping, limits, document numbering.
- **Payment system** and **transmission configurations** (SFTP, web service).
- **Disbursement bank accounts** and **payment documents** (check stock).
- **Funds disbursement process** run via PPRs.`, 'D'],

['Payments', 'How do you set up payment approval for large payment runs?',
`- Enable **payment approval** in payment options; define approval rules (BPM) by amount, currency, bank account.
- PPR stops for approval after build; approvers review and approve/reject payments.
- Also consider **"stop process for review"** settings for manual review of proposed payments.`, 'D'],

['Payments', 'How do you handle supplier refunds and outbound payments to non-suppliers?',
`- **Supplier refunds** (supplier pays you): record as a negative payment (refund) against a credit memo, or receipt in AR if appropriate.
- **Payment requests**: pay employees, customers (refunds) or one-time payees without creating suppliers — from AR refunds, Expenses or manual requests.`, 'D'],

['Expenses', 'How do corporate card expenses get from the card issuer to payment?',
`- Card issuer **transaction file** loaded daily → transactions matched to employees (card numbers).
- Employees add details/receipts and submit in expense reports.
- Approval and audit.
- **Payment**: company pays the card issuer (company pay) via AP invoice to the issuer; individual pay reimbursed to employees.`, 'D'],

['Expenses', 'How do expense audits work in Fusion?',
`- **Audit rules** select reports (by amount, policy violations, random sampling, specific employees/expense types).
- **Auditors** review receipts, adjust/reject lines, return to employees.
- **Receipt management**: track missing/received receipts; imaged receipts accepted.
- Audit list rules for repeat offenders.`, 'D'],

['Period Close', 'What is "sweep" in Payables close and when do you use it?',
`- At period close, unaccounted transactions (invoices/payments that can't be accounted in the period) can be **swept** to the next open period so the period can close.
- Use only after resolving what you can; review the swept list with accounting.`, 'B'],

['Interview', 'What is your approach to AP automation for a contract role?',
`- Assess current state: invoice channels, touchless rate, holds by type, cycle time, cost per invoice.
- Quick wins: tolerances, PO-level matching for BPAs, auto-approval of matched invoices, supplier data cleanup.
- Medium-term: IDR, supplier portal/e-invoicing, AI agents for hold triage and supplier inquiries.
- Measure monthly and report to the controller.`, 'B']
]);

QB['ora-mod-ap'] = (QB['ora-mod-ap'] || []).concat([["Accounts Payable","How would you architect an AP automation program end to end?","- **Inbound channels**: e-invoicing (EDI, PEPPOL, supplier portal), IDR for PDFs, paper scanning only as last resort.\n- **Validation**: PO matching, tolerances, duplicate detection, tax validation.\n- **Approval**: rules-based with auto-approval for matched invoices.\n- **AI agents** for exception handling (e.g. suggest resolution for holds).\n- **Payments**: optimized timing (discounts vs cash), virtual card programs.\n- **Analytics**: touchless rate, cycle time, cost per invoice.\n- **Supplier enablement** program as part of the architecture.","D"],["Accounts Payable","Explain the Procure-to-Pay flow and where AP fits in.","- **Requisition** → **Purchase Order** (Procurement)\n- **Receipt** of goods/services (Receiving)\n- **Invoice** entered/imported, matched to PO and receipt (AP)\n- **Validation** — matching, tax and holds checked (AP)\n- **Approval** (AP)\n- **Payment** through a Payment Process Request (AP / Payments)\n- **Accounting** — Create Accounting and transfer to GL (SLA)\n\nAccounting entries: receipt accrual (Dr Inventory/Expense, Cr Receipt Accrual), invoice (Dr Accrual, Cr Liability), payment (Dr Liability, Cr Cash Clearing), reconciliation (Dr Cash Clearing, Cr Cash).","B"],["Accounts Payable","What is the difference between 2-way, 3-way and 4-way matching?","- **2-way**: invoice vs PO (quantity and price). Used for services or low-risk items.\n- **3-way**: invoice vs PO vs **receipt** — you can only be invoiced for what you received.\n- **4-way**: invoice vs PO vs receipt vs **inspection/acceptance** — used where quality checks matter.\n\nThe match level is set on the PO schedule (defaulted from the supplier, item or purchasing options). Mismatches beyond tolerances put the invoice on hold.","B"],["Accounts Payable","What are invoice holds and how do you release them?","Holds stop an invoice from being paid until an issue is fixed.\n\n## Common holds\n- **Matching holds**: Qty Ord, Qty Rec, Price, Amount.\n- **Variance holds**: distribution/tax variance.\n- **Account holds**: invalid distribution account.\n- **Supplier/site holds**: payment hold on the supplier.\n- **Manual holds** placed by users.\n\n## Release\n- **System holds** release automatically when you fix the cause and re-validate (e.g. receive the goods, correct the price).\n- **Manual-releasable holds** are released by an authorized user with a release reason.\n- Holds can route to the right person via the holds resolution workflow.","B"],["Accounts Payable","What types of invoices can you enter in Payables?","- **Standard** — normal supplier invoice.\n- **Credit memo** — supplier credit (negative).\n- **Debit memo** — your own credit against a supplier.\n- **Prepayment** — advance payment, applied later to invoices.\n- **Withholding tax** — created automatically for withheld amounts.\n- **Expense report** — employee reimbursement (from Expenses).\n- **Payment request** — non-supplier payees (e.g. refunds).\n- **Interest** — late-payment interest (auto-created when set up).","B"],["Accounts Payable","How does a prepayment work in AP?","- Enter a **Prepayment** invoice, validate and pay it. Accounting: Dr Prepaid Expense, Cr Liability, then Cr Cash on payment.\n- When the real invoice arrives, **apply** the prepayment to it — this reduces the amount payable. Accounting reverses the prepaid asset into the invoice.\n- **Temporary** prepayments can be applied to invoices; **permanent** ones (e.g. deposits) cannot.\n- Prepayments can also be applied automatically at validation if set up.\n\nTip: Mention reconciling the prepaid account — unapplied prepayments are a common audit question.","B"],["Accounts Payable","What is a Payment Process Request (PPR)?","A PPR is how Fusion selects and pays invoices in bulk.\n\n- **Selection criteria**: pay-through date, business unit, supplier, payment method, pay group, currency.\n- **Payment and processing options**: which disbursement bank account, payment process profile, whether to stop for review.\n- Steps: **select invoices → review proposed payments → build payments → create payment file** (or print checks) → **transmit** to the bank.\n- Can be scheduled (e.g. weekly check run, daily ACH).\n\nPayment Process Profiles define the format (NACHA, ISO 20022 XML, check) and transmission method.","B"],["Accounts Payable","How do you handle 1099 reporting in Oracle Payables?","- Mark US suppliers as **federal reportable**, with taxpayer ID and income tax type (e.g. MISC, NEC).\n- Invoice distributions carry the **income tax type** — defaulted from the supplier.\n- During the year, fix errors with the **Update and Report Income Tax Details** process.\n- At year end, run the **1099 reports** (US 1099 Report, Invoice Exceptions, Supplier Exceptions) and produce the electronic file (1099 Electronic Media) or forms.\n\nTip: Mention reviewing the exception reports in December, not January.","D"],["Expenses","Explain the Oracle Expenses flow.","- Employee creates an **expense report** (mobile app or web), attaches receipts; corporate card transactions can import automatically.\n- **Expense policies** check limits (e.g. daily meal limit) and required receipts.\n- **Approval** by the manager (and others via rules).\n- **Auditor** review (sample or rule-based), receipt verification.\n- Approved reports become **Payables invoices** (Process Expense Reimbursements) and are paid via a PPR or payroll.\n- Accounting goes to the expense accounts defaulted from the employee's cost center and expense type.","B"],["Accounts Payable","What is the difference between a supplier, a supplier site and a site assignment?","- **Supplier** — the company you buy from (name, tax ID, type).\n- **Supplier address** — physical address.\n- **Supplier site** — the address used for purchasing and/or paying, created **per procurement business unit**, with payment terms, payment method, pay group and holds.\n- **Site assignment** — lets a **client business unit** use that site (e.g. a shared-services BU processes invoices for many BUs).\n\nSite assignments are a common reason a user \"can't find the supplier\" in a BU.","B"],["Accounts Payable","How do payment terms and discounts work in AP?","- **Payment terms** define due date(s) and early-payment discounts — e.g. **2/10 Net 30**: 2% discount if paid within 10 days, full amount due in 30.\n- Terms default from the supplier site (or PO) onto the invoice; the **terms date basis** (invoice date, goods received, system date) decides the starting date.\n- Installments are created from the terms; the PPR picks them up when due.\n- Discounts are taken automatically if the payment date falls within the discount period and the option is enabled.","B"],["Accounts Payable","What is the difference between invoice validation and invoice approval?","- **Validation** is a system check: matching to PO/receipt, tolerances, tax, accounting, distribution totals — it places or releases **holds**.\n- **Approval** is a business decision by people (managers, cost center owners) routed by approval rules.\n- An invoice usually needs **both** before it can be paid; the order (validate first or approve first) is configurable.","B"],["Accounts Payable","How do you cancel an invoice, and what happens to the accounting?","- You can cancel only **unpaid** invoices (void the payment first if paid) with no applied prepayments.\n- Cancel from the Invoices work area; Oracle reverses the distributions and creates **cancellation accounting** (reversing the liability and expense) on the cancel date.\n- If the invoice was matched to a PO, the PO quantity billed is reduced.\n- Cancelled invoices remain for audit; you can't undo a cancellation.","B"],["Accounts Payable","A supplier is paid twice. How would you investigate and prevent it from happening again?","## Investigate\n- Find both invoices: check invoice numbers, amounts, dates and supplier sites — duplicates often differ by a character, a leading zero or a different site.\n- Check the source: one manual and one from OCR/imaging or an integration is the most common pattern.\n- Check whether the duplicate-invoice validation was bypassed or the invoice number was altered.\n\n## Recover\n- Contact the supplier for a refund or credit memo; record it in AP and apply it, or net it on the next payment.\n- Make sure accounting and 1099/tax reporting stay correct.\n\n## Prevent\n- Tighten **duplicate-invoice checks** (supplier + number + amount + date) and normalize invoice numbers on import.\n- Use **holds and validation** consistently; control who can override.\n- Run a periodic duplicate-payment analytics report before each payment run.\n- Review the imaging/integration process so the same invoice can't enter by two routes.\n\nTip: Interviewers listen for root cause + control, not just \"we got the money back\".","D"],["Accounts Payable","How would you increase touchless invoice processing?","- Push suppliers to **PO-based invoicing** with accurate POs (catalogs, agreements).\n- Use **IDR** for capture and **supplier portal / e-invoicing** (EDI, PEPPOL) to remove paper.\n- Tune **matching tolerances** and use **receipt-based matching** where appropriate.\n- Fix master data (supplier sites, bank details, UOMs).\n- **Auto-approval** for PO-matched invoices within tolerance.\n- Analyze holds by type and supplier monthly; fix root causes.\n- KPI: touchless %, invoices per FTE, average cycle time.","D"],["Accounts Payable","How do you handle invoice matching for services or blanket POs where receipts are messy?","- For services, consider **2-way match** or **amount-based lines** instead of quantity.\n- For blanket POs with many receipts, consider moving the **invoice match option** from **Receipt** to **Purchase Order** (still 3-way) so AP doesn't have to pick specific receipts — Oracle checks received quantity in total.\n- Use **receipt acceptance** or **self-billing (pay on receipt)** for high-volume suppliers.\n- Train requesters to receive promptly.\n\nTrade-off: PO-level matching loses receipt-level traceability for price differences; agree with audit.","D"],["Expenses","How would you roll out Oracle Expenses to 5,000 employees?","- **Policies** harmonized across countries; travel policy owners sign off.\n- **Corporate card** feeds tested with the issuer; card holder matching.\n- **Mobile app** with receipt capture; single sign-on.\n- **Approval** via manager hierarchy (needs accurate HCM data).\n- **Pilot** with one department; fix issues; then waves.\n- Training: short videos and FAQs; help desk ready.\n- **Auditing**: risk-based audit rules to reduce manual audit.\n- Integration with payroll for reimbursements if used.","D"],["Accounts Payable","How would you design approvals for non-PO invoices across 15 business units?","- Base routing on **cost center manager hierarchy** (from HCM) with **amount tiers**.\n- BU-specific approval groups only where needed.\n- **Auto-approval** for small amounts/trusted suppliers with post-review.\n- **Fallback approver** when hierarchy data is missing.\n- Monitor approval cycle time and rejection rates; fix HCM data issues fast.","D"],["Accounts Payable","How do you configure invoice approval workflow in Fusion Payables?","- Enable **invoice approval** in Manage Invoice Options.\n- Define rules in **BPM** (Manage Invoice Approval Rules), e.g.:\n- PO-matched invoices within tolerance → no approval;\n- non-PO invoices → cost center manager by amount;\n- over $100k → add controller.\n- Use **approval groups** or supervisor hierarchy; handle missing approvers with a fallback.\n- Decide whether approval happens **before or after validation** and whether payment requires approval.\n- Test reassign, delegate, request information and rejection flows.","D"],["Accounts Payable","What is Intelligent Document Recognition (IDR) / invoice imaging?","- Suppliers email invoices (PDF/images) to a dedicated address.\n- Oracle's **IDR** (AI-based capture) reads the invoice and creates an **incomplete invoice** with header and line data, matched to PO when possible.\n- AP staff review and complete exceptions in the **Invoices work area**.\n- Higher accuracy over time as it learns supplier layouts.\n\n## What I'd watch\n- Supplier communication and email rules.\n- Duplicate detection (invoice sent by email and mail).\n- KPIs: touchless rate, processing time.","D"],["Payments","How do you set up a new payment format, for example a bank's ACH file?","- Get the bank's **file specification** and test process.\n- Use or copy a seeded **BI Publisher template** (e.g. NACHA, ISO 20022 pain.001) and adjust for the bank's requirements.\n- Create the **payment format** pointing to the template.\n- Create a **payment process profile** linking format, payment method, transmission (SFTP/ web service) and grouping rules.\n- Assign the profile to the **disbursement bank account** and payment methods.\n- Test with the bank: prenotes, small live payments, acknowledgment files.","D"],["Payments","What is positive pay?","A fraud-prevention service: you send the bank a file of issued checks (number, amount, payee). The bank only pays checks that match.\n- In Fusion, run the **Positive Pay File** process (with a BIP template in the bank's format) after each check run.\n- Includes voided checks.\n- Transmit automatically via SFTP.\n- Exceptions are reviewed by treasury before the bank pays.","B"],["Payments","How do you void a payment and what happens to the invoices and accounting?","- **Void** the payment in Payables with a void date and action for the invoices: **cancel**, **place on hold**, or **leave open** for repayment.\n- Accounting reverses the payment entry (Dr Cash Clearing, Cr Liability) on the void date.\n- If the payment cleared the bank, you can't simply void — handle via stop payment or refund instead.\n- For electronic payments, void only if the bank rejected the payment.\n- Watch 1099 impact and positive pay files (send voids).","D"],["Expenses","How do you configure expense policies and corporate cards?","- **Expense templates and types** (meals, hotel, mileage) with default accounts.\n- **Policies** — rate limits (by location, job level), daily limits, receipt requirements, justification thresholds; policy warnings vs errors.\n- **Mileage rates** by period/vehicle.\n- **Corporate cards**: card program, card issuer file loading (e.g. Amex, Visa feeds), matching to employees, payment options (company pay vs individual pay).\n- **Audit rules** and receipt management.","D"],["Accounts Payable","How do you prevent supplier bank account fraud in Oracle?","- Restrict who can **create or change supplier bank accounts** (separate from AP processing).\n- Require **bank account verification** — supplier portal changes go through **approval** (supplier profile change requests).\n- Callback procedures to a known contact before approving changes.\n- Audit reports on bank account changes before each payment run.\n- Hold first payments to a new account for review.\n\nTip: Mention a real control and the SoD conflicts you checked.","D"],["Accounts Payable","What are the key Payables options and invoice options you configure?","- **Invoice options**: invoice approval, allow adjustments to paid invoices, duplicate invoice checking, invoice currency, receipt acceptance days, default payment terms basis, require validation before approval.\n- **Matching**: tolerances, allow distribution override, recalculation on match.\n- **Payment options**: allow payment date before system date, bank charge bearer, payment document options.\n- **Accounting option**: accrual or cash basis, automatic offsets, prepayment account.\n- **Self-service invoices** and supplier portal options.\n\nEach is set by business unit.","D"],["Accounts Payable","How do you set up and use payment terms with installments?","- Define the payment terms with **multiple lines**: e.g. 50% due in 30 days, 50% due in 60 days, with optional discounts per line.\n- Invoices using the terms get **installments** created automatically, each with its own due date and amount.\n- The payment process picks up each installment when due.\n- Useful for large purchases and contracts with staged payments.","D"],["Expenses","How do you handle an employee who submitted an expense report with a policy violation?","- Policy rules flag the line (warning or error).\n- Warnings may require a **justification** from the employee; errors block submission.\n- The manager sees violations during approval; **auditors** can reject or adjust lines.\n- Repeat issues → training or tighter rules; report on violations by department.","B"]]);
